.TH WRIT\-ROTATE 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-rotate \- replace the keys of an identity, keeping its id
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit rotate\fR \fB\-i\fR\ \fIDIR\fR \fB\-\-next\-key\fR\ \fIPATH\fR... \fB\-\-next\-out\fR\ \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-\-next\-n\fR\ \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-next\-k\fR\ \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-next\-pub\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-max\-validity\fR\ \fIDUR\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-max\-validity\fR] [\fB\-q\fR]
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Rotate the identity in DIR. The next keys (\-\-next\-key) sign the rotation and become the current keys. writ makes new next keys and writes them to \-\-next\-out. The id does not change.
.PP
A rotation lists the statements that stay valid. writ keeps every statement in DIR/issued.writ that is still valid: not revoked, not expired, and not a grant to a leaf key. Anything else signed with the old keys becomes invalid for every verifier that sees the rotation. Use this to recover from a stolen current key.
.PP
If DIR/current.key exists, writ replaces it with the key you revealed. If not, give the revealed key with \-\-key from now on.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
the identity to rotate
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
a next key from the last rotation (or from init); give one \-\-next\-key per key; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the new next key; '\-' means standard output
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-n\fR \fIN\fR
how many new next keys to make (default: 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-k\fR \fIK\fR
how many next keys must sign the next rotation (default: 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-pub\fR \fIPATH\fR
add another person's public key (from \fBwrit pubkey\fR) to the next keys
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-validity\fR \fIDUR\fR
set a new limit on how long statements may last
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-max\-validity\fR
remove the limit on how long statements may last
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print nothing on success
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Rotate, with the next keys encrypted by age:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ rotate \-i me \-\-next\-key <(age \-d me\-next.key.age) \-\-next\-out \- | age \-p > me\-next2.key.age
.fi
.RE
